uating a retriever, including setting up a testing environment, defining success criteria, and measuring metrics like recall and precision.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context explains that the success criteria for the retriever involves retrieving relevant documents, which aligns with the evaluation metrics discussed.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context describes the steps involved in evaluating a retriever, including configuring parameters and logging results, which matches the information provided in the retrieval context.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context talks about improving the performance of the retriever by increasing recall and precision, which is consistent with the evaluation metrics mentioned.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Vibe testing is a form of informal, subjective, and non-repetitive evaluation of an LLM (Large Language Model) application. It involves asking five questions and evaluating the application based on its response. The main purpose of vibe testing is to assess the effectiveness and reliability of the LLM application. While it can provide valuable insights into how well the LLM is performing, it is not sufficient for deploying an LLM app because it lacks the rigors required for production-grade software development.\\nThe reasons why vibe testing is not enough for deploying an LLM app include:\\n\\n1. Lack of reproducibility: Since the vibe testing process\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what is vibe testing and why isn't it enough for deploying an llm app?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"applications. But if I ask the next question, have you ever evaluated it properly? Have you ever made an application or a chatbot? Have you ever evaluated it methodologically? So what would you say? I feel a lot of you will say that sir, I haven't evaluated it properly. But I asked him questions and I found the answer to it. So I assumed that this application is working properly. Right? Mostly we do this. What we do is we build an LLME-based application. Then before checking it, before launching it, before sharing it with someone, we ask the 4-5 questions that come to our mind at that point. And that's it. If the right responses come there, then we accept that our application is working properly. Actually, this whole phenomenon, this way of testing, this term has been given in the industry. It is called vibe testing. Just like vibe coding, it is vibe testing. What is vibe testing? Look, I have written it here. Vibe testing means you casually trying an LLME application with a few\", \"coding, it is vibe testing. What is vibe testing? Look, I have written it here. Vibe testing means you casually trying an LLME application with a few prompts and judging it by feel. You asked 5 questions and read the answers. And judge based on the feel whether it is right or not. This is called vibe testing. So basically what you do is you ask 5-10 questions, the answer is right, you feel that it is working. It is informal, it is subjective, and it is usually not repeatable. Because today you asked 5 questions, after 10 days you won't ask the same 5 questions, you will ask something else. So it is not even repeatable. Now you must be understanding even after listening to vibe testing that it works only at the personal project level. By doing vibe testing, you don't make production grade software in the industry. Obviously. Right? Now I will tell you some instances, some case studies where teams made a mistake that they deployed a LLME based application by doing vibe testing. And I\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.16666666666666666, "reason": "The score is 0.17 because the actual output does not match any of the provided contradictions.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Vibe testing involves casually trying an LLME application with a few prompts and judging its functionality by feel.\",\n    \"Vibe testing is informal, subjective, and typically not repeatable due to changing user needs over time.\",\n    \"Teams sometimes deploy LLME applications incorrectly using vibe testing methods without proper evaluation.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"Vibe testing is a form of informal, subjective, and non-repetitive evaluation of an LLM (Large Language Model) application.\",\n    \"It involves asking five questions and evaluating the application based on its response.\",\n    \"The main purpose of vibe testing is to assess the effectiveness and reliability of the LLM application.\",\n    \"While it can provide valuable insights into how well the LLM is performing, it is not sufficient for deploying an LLM app because it lacks the rigors required for production-grade software development.\",\n    \"The reasons why vibe testing is not enough for deploying an LLM app include:\",\n    \"Lack of reproducibility: Since the vibe testing process cannot be replicated consistently due to the subjective nature of the evaluations.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score cannot be higher because the question asks specifically about VIBE testing and deployment strategies for an LLM application, which aligns directly with the provided information. The score reflects the relevance of the response to the given query.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Vibe testing is a form of informal, subjective, and non-repetitive evaluation of an LLM (Large Language Model) application.\",\n    \"It involves asking five questions and evaluating the application based on its response.\",\n    \"The main purpose of vibe testing is to assess the effectiveness and reliability of the LLM application.\",\n    \"While it can provide valuable insights into how well the LLM is performing, it is not sufficient for deploying an LLM app because it lacks the rigors required for production-grade software development.\",\n    \"The reasons why vibe testing is not enough for deploying an LLM app include:\",\n    \"Lack of reproducibility: Since the vibe testing process\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement introduces the concept of vibe testing.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement explains what vibe testing entails.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement describes the primary goal of vibe testing.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement provides examples of the questions asked during vibe testing.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement elaborates on the limitations of vibe testing compared to production-grade software development.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"The MMLU benchmark was introduced in 2025 as a new benchmark to evaluate the performance of large language models (LLMs). Initially, it was designed to replace MMLU, which was considered a benchmark due to its popularity and saturation. Over time, the benchmark became saturated, leading to the development of various benchmarks like AGI eval, GPQA, and MMLU Pro. These benchmarks were gradually replaced with simpler ones like truthfull QA and humanity's last exam. The evolution of benchmarks reflects the increasing complexity and sophistication of language models, aiming to better assess their capabilities and limitations.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what is the mmlu benchmark?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"but in MMLU Pro every question had 10 options so here you have to find the right answer but in 10 given options so you can see that the difficulty level increased after this what they did is they reduced the number of subjects from 57 they came down if I remember correctly they came down to 12 subjects and in 12 subjects they got 12k questions so per subject 1000 questions and they added some such questions which will give some reasoning so overall they made MMLU a little difficult so this for the next 1-2 years it was a good benchmark on which people kept testing their models basically MMLU Pro replaced MMLU but as it always happens that every next generation model beats the previous benchmark so over time what happened AGI eval also kind of saturated in GPQA also kind of near saturation and finally in 2025 a new benchmark came which is very popular and it is still used it's name is HLE Humanities Last Exam I can understand from the name how dangerous it is so in this people did that\", \"came then when MMLU was saturated people thought that we should test it with breadth of knowledge so then GPQA came and people thought that MMLU is a good benchmark we should repair it so in the process of repairing MMLU Pro came then slowly AGI eval, GPQA and MMLU Pro when all three of them started to saturate then finally people thought that we should make a new benchmark in which we will keep breadth and depth and some more innovations so we will keep it and call it humanity's last exam and this benchmark is still going on and when truthfull QA was saturated then we replaced it with simple QA so in total there are seven benchmarks that we are going to discuss today MMLU, truthfull QA AGI eval, GPQA MMLU Pro, simple QA humanity's last exam these are the seven benchmarks that we will discuss in detail but I have already told you an evolution story that why all these benchmarks came in picture and when they came in picture so let me know if this discussion made sense to you or if you\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.4, "reason": "The score is 0.40 because the actual output does not fully align with the retrieval context on several points related to MMLU's replacement and benchmarking strategies.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"MMLU Pro replaced MMLU due to its saturation.\",\n    \"AGI eval became popular as a new benchmark in 2025.\",\n    \"HLE (Humanity's Last Exam) is a benchmark that continues to be used despite being saturated.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"The MMLU benchmark was introduced in 2025 as a new benchmark to evaluate the performance of large language models (LLMs).\",\n    \"Initially, it was designed to replace MMLU, which was considered a benchmark due to its popularity and saturation.\",\n    \"Over time, the benchmark became saturated, leading to the development of various benchmarks like AGI eval, GPQA, and MMLU Pro.\",\n    \"These benchmarks were gradually replaced with simpler ones like truthfull QA and humanity's last exam.\",\n    \"The evolution of benchmarks reflects the increasing complexity and sophistication of language models, aiming to better assess their capabilities and limitations.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"The claim about MMLU being replaced by MMLU Pro contradicts the information that MMLU was initially designed to replace MMLU.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"The claim about HLE becoming a benchmark despite being saturated does not directly contradict the context regarding MMLU and AGI eval.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"The claim about these benchmarks being replaced with simpler ones does not directly contradict the context regarding MMLU and AGI eval.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 0.8333333333333334, "reason": "The score is 0.83 because some irrelevant statements were present in the actual output, such as 'This statement does not provide direct relevance to the question asked.'", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"MMLU benchmark was introduced in 2025.\",\n    \"It replaced MMLU due to its popularity and saturation.\",\n    \"Over time, the benchmark became saturated.\",\n    \"Various benchmarks like AGI eval, GPQA, and MMLU Pro were developed.\",\n    \"These benchmarks were gradually replaced by simpler ones like truthfull QA and humanity's last exam.\",\n    \"The evolution of benchmarks reflects the increasing complexity and sophistication of language models.\",\n    \"Language models' capabilities and limitations are being assessed through these benchmarks.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement provides context about the introduction and development of benchmarks.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement discusses the replacement of one benchmark with another over time.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement mentions various benchmarks that have been developed.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement explains how benchmarks reflect the evolving capabilities and limitations of language models.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement does not provide direct relevance to the question asked.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"To better understand how MMLU Pro improved on the original MMLU, let's break down the process step by step:\\n\\n1. **Initial Setup**: The original MMLU (Modeling Language Model) was designed to evaluate models' performance on various types of tasks. It aimed to create a comprehensive evaluation framework that could handle a wide range of scenarios.\\n\\n2. **Incremental Improvement**: As the project progressed, researchers began to refine the evaluation metrics and algorithms. They realized that traditional single-choice multiple choice (MCQ) tests were insufficient for capturing the nuanced understanding and reasoning abilities of models.\\n\\n3. **Change in Question Types\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how did mmlu pro improve on the original mmlu?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"models are reaching humans in MMLU Pro there was no such human baseline in the paper they did not give it, whereas in the original MMLU, people criticized that the benchmark you have designed why are you not carrying the core philosophy that's it, it is not a big point shall we move to the next ok the next benchmark is interesting, in the sense that the benchmarks we discussed earlier are different in two aspects we will discuss both the aspects, before that I will tell you the story of this benchmark so if you remember, we discussed that everything started from MMLU one branch came out of it that take the exam like humans quickly tell us which benchmark was it, quick quiz in between just to check if you are retaining or not will anyone tell us which benchmark was it that said rather than creating new benchmarks why don't you use existing exams to check model intelligence or their knowledge which was it come on, no one remembers AGI well AGI well then who asked questions about\", \"so whatever problems were there in MM LU they tried to solve them let me tell you what things were solved the first change is that instead of giving 4 options in MCQ we give 10 options what will happen if we give 10 options according to you can you tell me if you are going to give one exam let's say IIT JEE or whatever other exams and instead of 4 options you get 10 options in every question so will your exam become more difficult or easy or I guess you can understand so we have given such exams and we try to eliminate options and reach the right option so when you have 4 options then elimination is easy you have to eliminate 3 and reach 1 when you have 10 then it will become very difficult so basically to increase the complexity researchers gave 10 options instead of 4 second, what they did is they removed the most basic questions and the noisy questions from MM LU and instead of them added reasoning based questions I will show you an example after a while but what did they do? MM LU\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.5, "reason": "The score is 0.50 because the retrieval context supports both claims.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Researchers changed the number of options from 4 to 10 in multiple choice questions (MCQ) to increase difficulty.\",\n    \"They also reduced basic and noisy questions in MM LU and replaced them with reasoning-based questions in MMLU Pro.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"MMLU Pro improved upon the original MMLU by enhancing its ability to evaluate models comprehensively.\",\n    \"Researchers refined the evaluation metrics and algorithms to address limitations of traditional MCQ tests.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context supports both claims.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context directly contradicts the first claim.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score cannot be higher because there are no irrelevant statements in the actual output regarding MMLU's improvement.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"The initial setup of MMLU focused on evaluating models across different tasks.\",\n    \"Researchers made improvements to the evaluation metrics and algorithms.\",\n    \"They identified MCQ tests as inadequate for assessing models' understanding and reasoning capabilities.\",\n    \"MMLU evolved into a more sophisticated system capable of handling varied question types.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"Improvements were made to the evaluation metrics and algorithms.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"Identified MCQ tests as inadequate for assessing models' understanding and reasoning capabilities.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"Evolved into a more sophisticated system capable of handling varied question types.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Sure! Let me provide you with a real-world example of an LLM (Large Language Model) application failing badly.\\n\\n### Example Scenario: Airbnb Chatbot Incident\\n\\nIn the past few years, several incidents involving Airbnb's chatbot have highlighted significant issues with how these AI-powered tools handle user interactions. One notable incident involved a tragic case where a passenger on an Airbnb flight had to cancel their booking due to a misunderstanding regarding bereavement fair policies. \\n\\nThe passenger, who was traveling with her grandmother, was looking for bereavement fair information on Airbnb's website. However, upon arriving at the airport, she found out that the airline\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"can you give a real-world example of an llm application failing badly?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"I will tell you some instances, some case studies where teams made a mistake that they deployed a LLME based application by doing vibe testing. And I will tell you the results with the help of 3 case studies. The first case study is Air Canada's chatbot. This was a very popular incident around 2-3 years ago. Where there was this guy and his grandmother died. And he was searching for bereavement fair on Air Canada's website. Bereavement fair is basically when someone in your family dies or your close friend dies and you have to travel, then airlines give you a discount because you are in a bad situation. I am not sure if this happens in India but it happens in Canada. So this guy went and on Air Canada's website he chatted with the chatbot about the bereavement fair policy. Now at this point the chatbot hallucinated and instead of telling the correct policy, he said that you book a ticket now, pay the full amount and travel. And later we will refund your entire amount. So this guy\", \"the correct policy, he said that you book a ticket now, pay the full amount and travel. And later we will refund your entire amount. So this guy booked the ticket and travelled. And after travelling when he tried to refund, customer support said that such a policy does not exist. We have this policy that you can take discounts before travelling. We do not refund anything later. So this guy got upset and filed a case against Air Canada. In the court, Air Canada's executives and lawyers pitched that the chatbot is a separate entity. Whatever he is saying, Air Canada does not know about it. It is like a separate entity. The judge said that this is not allowed. If your website is your property, then your chatbot is also your property. Which means that whatever your chatbot says, you will have to take its responsibility. Which means you will have to refund. So around 600 dollars of refund was to be done to Air Canada, which is not a big amount but it was a big blow on the name of a big\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions between the actual outpu