act your user can also drift I am giving a very bad example let's say suddenly millennials used your platform and suddenly gen z's started using so from the\", \"user can also drift I am giving a very bad example let's say suddenly millennials used your platform and suddenly gen z's started using so from the expectation point of view there is a drift that what is he expecting, he is not getting the answer he is giving thumbs down now your current offline evaluation everything is working fine but your user who was a millennial before is not happy with it so that is also a drift which you have to see I don't know I haven't given a good example drift can be of many types we were talking about data drift but model drift and I am trying to think what other types of drift can be concept drift what I have discussed is concept drift that expectation mismatch concept has changed that chart board was used for some other purpose and now it is used for some other purpose so you can drift from any type so drift you cannot control I don't think you can control drift in any AI based system some form of drift gets in any other question guys if there is no\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.5, "reason": "The score is 0.50 because the actual output partially contradicts the retrieval context due to minor discrepancies that do not significantly impact overall alignment.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Drift can occur due to changes in expectations, usage patterns, or underlying data characteristics.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"Drift refers to the difference between the expected outcomes and the actual results during the training process.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"re