 {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"The question asks why we use an LLM (Large Language Model) as a judge instead of simply comparing strings. The provided context discusses the challenges and improvements in LLMs' performance in recent years, particularly in terms of their ability to accurately extract answers from text. This aligns with the idea that LLMs can handle more complex and nuanced tasks compared to traditional string-based methods. Therefore, the answer would likely involve discussing the advantages and limitations of using LLMs as judges, emphasizing their improved performance and the need for careful consideration of the specific task at hand.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"why do we use an llm as a judge instead of simple string matching?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"data set has become a part of their alignment training data so contamination is in that sense, not in pre-training, but in alignment stage after that disputed gold labels so here we have how many questions around 817 questions, among them there are many questions about people's disputes that this is not a misconception, this is correct so that kind of affects it and lastly deprecated GPT judge, breaks, cross or comparison I forgot to tell you this so I told you that there are three ways to measure this, one is generation where model A, B, C or D is printing or generating by itself, second is MC1 where you are doing log probabilities MC2 is you are doing log probabilities against the right ones and then joining the right ones, I told you three ways in the generated model you are getting A, B, C, D which is available to analyze this answer you use an LLM as a judge because anything can be printed here this can also be the answer is A or it can be printed as A is the answer so you have\", \"you use an LLM as a judge because anything can be printed here this can also be the answer is A or it can be printed as A is the answer so you have to extract A from all kinds of scenarios so to do this you use an LLM so when this benchmark was released in the beginning they used a version of GPT-4 as a LLM as a judge now what happened is new models came forward and people started using LLM as a judge so there was an issue in performance that the previous generation might have made a mistake in extracting A in some questions so due to that the accuracy came down but the later LLM as a judge they are better, they are able to extract each answer correctly so their accuracy is a little higher so in that sense there is a problem here, you will see this pattern whenever you are using LLM as a judge so over time since LLM's performance is improving so is the performance of LLM as a judge so due to that there is a little discrepancy today the measurement you are taking you can't compare it\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions between the actual output and the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"LLM as a judge can extract answers incorrectly.\",\n    \"Performance improvement of LLMs leads to improved performance of LLM as a judge.\",\n    \"Contamination during alignment training data affects the measurement of LLM as a judge.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"LLMs have improved their ability to accurately extract answers from text over time.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold"