ight want to sample conversations that show signs of engagement, such as thumbs down, positive feedback, or escalation. By focusing on these types of conversations, you can ensure that your evaluation process remains fair and unbiased. Additionally, you can also sample conversations that indicate dissatisfaction, such as negative feedback or complaints. This helps you identify patterns and trends in user behavior, allowing you to make informed decisions about how to improve your service. Sampling ensures that your evaluation process is efficient and effective, leading to better outcomes for both users\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how does sampling work in online evaluation?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"up more samples from those categories and less samples from normal ones. So from this the sampled conversations I will get, there is more chance of hallucination being detected. So I have discussed the same thing with you. We are doing clustering. You will have to set a small model for this. A small model will detect which one has thumbs down, which one is talking like this. So this is technicality. I just wanted to tell you that you don't do normal sampling you do stratified sampling. So is the point clear guys? So far the flow I have told you is correct. We have locked it. After that we did sampling. After that we set up our evaluator. Evaluator performs the evaluation on those sampled conversations and gives us the metric. We are aggregating that metric over a time period and showing it on the dashboard. And if a threshold is crossing then I am triggering the alerting system. This is the entire flow for online eval. There is one evaluation pipeline. You set up multiple evaluators\", \"use of keeping those conversations? We will not know that. What about we focus more on those questions where we got thumbs down? Will it be a good strategy? Think about it. Will it be a good strategy if we focus more on those questions where we got thumbs down? Right? Or we focus on those conversations which suddenly got over abruptly or where escalations happened. Or where I don't know the user is rephrasing the same question in different ways. Or where we are talking about money, refund, admission, fees. So basically what I am trying to say is that not all conversations are the same. What you do is you do stratified sampling. Stratified sampling. You basically divide all your conversations into categories. And then you pick up more samples in problematic categories. Like we are talking about money or thumbs down. So I will pick up more samples from those categories and less samples from normal ones. So from this the sampled conversations I will get, there is more chance of\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 0.8333333333333334, "reason": "The score is 0.83 because the retrieval context does not directly contradict the claim.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Stratified sampling involves dividing conversations into categories and picking more samples from problematic categories such as money-related issues or questions with thumbs down.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"Sampling works by dividing your dataset into smaller subsets (called samples) based on certain criteria.\",\n    \"In the context of online evaluation, you might want to sample conversations that show signs of engagement, such as thumbs down, positive feedback, or escalation.\",\n    \"By focusing on these types of conversations, you can ensure that your evaluation process remains fair and unbiased.\",\n    \"Additionally, you can also sample conversations that indicate dissatisfaction, such as negative feedback or complaints.\",\n    \"This helps you identify patterns and trends in user behavior, allowing you to make informed 