yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement explains what sampling is and its importance in online evaluations.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement provides additional context about how sampling works in online evaluations.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement discusses the benefits of using sampling in online evaluations.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement highlights the advantages of using sampling in online evaluations.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"In the context of online evaluation systems like LangSmith, there are two main types of signals that you need to consider: computed signals and captured signals. Computed signals are those that you have to calculate or figure out based on the input data. These signals typically involve processing text, analyzing sentiment, or extracting specific patterns from the conversation. Captured signals, on the other hand, are pre-existing pieces of information stored within the system. They can include things like timestamps, keywords, mentions of entities, or even the overall flow of the conversation. \\n\\nFor example, if a user says \\\"thumbs up\\\" during a conversation, this would be\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what are computed and captured signals in online eval?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"not? Okay? So there are actually two types of signals which you have to focus on. The first one is computed and the second one is captured signals. Okay? So computed signals are those that you have to calculate or figure out. And captured signals are those that are already present and you have to store them somewhere. I will give you both the examples. For example, captured signal is thumbs up, thumbs down. If the user did thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation, then this is a signal that the user is giving me. So I am directly storing it in the langsmith. Now I didn't need to calculate anything here. The user said thumbs down, I have to store it as it is. So we call this captured signal. One more example is latency. To do any particular answer, how much time did my chatbot take? Whatever time it took, I have to store it in the langsmith. Now I don't need to calculate anything here. Cost per conversation. How much token did I spend? How much money did I spend? Again, it is\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.5, "reason": "The score is 0.50 because none of the provided contradictions indicate any discrepancies between the actual output and the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Latency refers to the time taken by a chatbot to respond to a query.\",\n    \"Cost per conversation involves tracking the number of tokens used and the monetary cost incurred.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"Computed signals refer to processed elements derived from input data.\",\n    \"Captured signals encompass existing data points within the system.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"None\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieved context directly contradicts the first claim.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score cannot be higher because there are no irrelevant statements in the actual output regarding what are computed and captured signals in online evaluation.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Computed signals involve calculating or figuring out based on input data.\",\n    \"Captured signals are pre-existing pieces of information stored within the system.\",\n    \"An example of a computed signal could be detecting 'thumbs up' during a conversation.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement provides context about what computed and captured signals are.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"A benchmark becomes saturated when it loses its relevance and usefulness due to the rapid improvement of models. In the context provided, a benchmark like GTSDB (Google Test Suite Benchmark) or Opus 9.5 is considered saturated because it consistently produces results close to the best possible scores. As models improve, the gap between the benchmark's expected performance and the actual performance decreases, leading to a gradual decline in the benchmark's accuracy. This process is known as benchmark saturation, which occurs when the benchmark is no longer effective in differentiating models based on their performance.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what does it mean for a benchmark to get saturated?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"the same place so there is no difference in score between them opus 9.5 your most recent model is at 95% gpt 5.6 is at 94% google gemini is at 92% so basically there are many clustering at the same place this is called benchmark saturation this means that this b