oes not provide specific details about storing information.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement does not specify which type of information should be stored.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement does not mention how the information should be stored.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement does not indicate where the information should be stored.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"The user is asking about the importance of thumbs up and thumbs down signals in building a golden dataset for online evaluation systems. To elaborate, the golden dataset is a set of test cases designed to evaluate the performance of different models. It includes both positive (thumbs up) and negative (thumbs down) responses to ensure fairness and consistency in the evaluation process. Thumbs up and thumbs down signals play a crucial role in creating a balanced and representative dataset. Positive responses indicate that the model performed well, which helps in identifying areas where improvements could be made. Negative responses, however, serve as a baseline measure, helping to determine whether the model is performing\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how can thumbs up and thumbs down signals help build a golden dataset?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"not? Is there any other signal that can tell this instead of the correct answer? Let me tell you. What if you have given the option of thumbs up and thumbs down in your chatbot? If you suddenly start seeing that in the last one hour, the rating of thumbs down is coming in a lot of conversations, then what will you understand from this? That something or the other has gone wrong. Something or the other is giving the wrong answer to our chatbot. So again, this is a tricky way to find out that is the correctness working properly or not? Yes, basically the user's feedback has been done. So here the correctness has been alternated by the user's feedback. Yeah Aman, we judge LLM as a judge. I will show you now. Look, understand one thing. One important thing is that the questions you are asking are a little ahead of the questions. Because we have not discussed the how part of online evals yet. Technically, we just discussed that why do we need online evals and what exactly are online evals?\", \"store the question that the user asked. What context was generated in the rag chat bot in the process of answering that question? I will store that context in the chat bot. I will store the output that the chat bot gave on the basis of that context and question. Apart from that, I will store a lot of operational things such as latency in milliseconds, how many tokens were spent in the prompt, how many completion tokens are there, how much total cost was spent, did any error come? If an error came, what is its status code? I will store all this. Apart from this, if the user is giving me some other signals, I will store those signals too. Like if there is a behavioral metric such as he is pressing thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation, then I will store that too. Is he mailing? He is saying that I don't want to talk to you, I want to talk to a human or give my email ID, I want to mail. So if the matter is escalating, I will store that. The user is asking the same questions\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.3333333333333333, "reason": "The score is 0.33 because the actual output does not match any contradiction provided and lacks specific details that would justify a higher score.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "ver