 this statement provides additional context about the importance of positive and negative responses, it does not directly relate to the specific question asked.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"A step-by-step prompt helps an LLM (Large Language Model) solve a math problem by breaking down complex operations into smaller, manageable steps. This approach allows the model to focus on solving individual components before combining them to arrive at the final result. Here\\u2019s how it works:\\n\\n1. **Division**: The model starts by dividing the total number of clips (48) by the number of clips sold per unit (2). This gives us the number of units sold.\\n   - \\\\( \\\\frac{48}{2} = 24 \\\\)\\n\\n2. **Multiplication**: Next, the model multiplies the number of units\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how does a step-by-step prompt help an llm solve a math problem?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"kind of system prompt that see this question and try to solve it step by step like here the answer is also shown Natalia sold 48 divided by 2 means 24 clips in may Natalia sold 48 plus 24 clips all together this is getting solved step by step and is reaching the answer if you are not allowing chain of thought and you are telling to directly answer this then chances of mistakes are more so in GSM 8k also chain of thought is allowed again, here we are just talking about the options available while constructing the prompt few shot, zero shot, chain of thought or normal after that you see things in your decoding and sampling configuration you keep temperature value around zero because if you increase it more than zero then creative answers start coming and every time they start varying so temperature is mostly around zero for such benchmarks then you set a max token if you set a very less max token then it can be since chain of thought is triggered and while thinking in the reach the\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.5, "reason": "The score is 0.50 because none of the provided contradictions indicate any discrepancies between the actual output and the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Chain of thought is allowed in GSM 8K benchmarks.\",\n    \"Temperature should be kept around zero when setting max tokens.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"A step-by-step prompt helps an LLM solve a math problem by breaking down complex operations into smaller, manageable steps.\",\n    \"The model divides the total number of clips by the number of clips sold per unit to find the number of units sold.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"None\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context directly contradicts the first claim by stating that a step-by-step prompt helps an LLM solve a math problem.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score cannot be higher because the question asks how a step-by-step prompt helps an LLM solve a math problem, which directly addresses the relevance of the provided information. The score remains at 1.00 as no additional irrelevant content was found.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"A step-by-step prompt helps an LLM solve a math problem by breaking down complex operations into smaller, manageable steps.\",\n    \"This approach allows the model to focus on solving individual components before combining them to arrive at the final result.\",\n    \"The model starts by dividing the total number of clips (48) by the number of clips sold per unit (2). This gives us the number of units sold.\",\n    \"The number of units sold is calculated as \\( \\frac{48}{2} = 24 \\)\",\n    \"Next, the model multiplies the number of units sold by another factor (which is not specified in the given text).\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The first two statements provide examples of how a step-by-step prompt can help an LLM solve a math problem.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The third statement provides a calculation that aligns with the concept of using a step-by-step prompt.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The fourth statement shows a specific example of calculating the number of units sold using a step-by-step approach.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The fifth statement introduces a multiplier which is not specified in the original text, making it ambiguous.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"In online evaluation, both computed and captured signals play crucial roles. Computed signals are data points that you derive from analyzing the conversation between the user and the AI model. These signals can include things like the frequency of certain keywords, the length of specific sentences, or the type of questions asked. They allow you to understand patterns and trends within the conversation, helping you tailor future interactions effectively.\\n\\nCaptured signals, on the other hand, are data points that are already stored in the AI model itself. This means they represent the outcomes of previous conversations, such as the number of times a particular phrase was used, the duration of a particular interaction\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what are computed and captured signals in online evaluation?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"not? Okay? So there are actually two types of signals which you have to focus on. The first one is computed and the second one is captured signals. Okay? So computed signals are those that you have to calculate or figure out. And captured signals are those that are already present and you have to store them somewhere. I will give you both the examples. For example, captured signal is thumbs up, thumbs down. If the user did thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation, then this is a signal that the user is giving me. So I am directly storing it in the langsmith. Now I didn't need to calculate anything here. The user said thumbs down, I have to store it as it is. So we call this captured signal. One more example is latency. To do any particular answer, how much time did my chatbot take? Whatever time it took, I have to store it in the langsmith. Now I don't need to calculate anything here. Cost per conversation. How much token did I spend? How much money did I spend? Again, it is\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.6666666666666666, "reason": "The score is 0.67 because the actual output partially contradicts the retrieval context due to minor discrepancies in details and assumptions.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Latency refers to the time taken by a chatbot to respond to a query.\",\n    \"Cost per conversation involves tracking the number of tokens used and the monetary cost incurred.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"Both computed and captured signals are important in understanding and tailoring AI interactions.\",\n    \"Computed signals involve deriving new data from user-AI interactions.\",\n    \"Captured signals refer to outcomes from past interactions that the AI has learned.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieved context directly contradicts the first claim by stating that latency refers to the time taken by a chatbot to respond to a query.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieved context directly contradicts the second claim by stating that Cost per conversation involves tracking the number of tokens used and the monetary cost incurred.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score cannot be higher because there are no irrelevant statements in the actual output regarding the question about computed and captured signals in online evaluation.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Both computed and captured signals are important in online evaluations.\",\n    \"Computed signals come from analyzing the conversation between users and AI models.\",\n    \"They include factors like keyword frequencies and sentence lengths.\",\n    \"Captured signals are outcomes from past conversations stored in the AI model.\",\n    \"These outcomes help tailor future interactions effectively.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement addresses the importance of both computed and captured signals in online evaluations.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement explains what computed signals are derived from and includes examples of their components.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement clarifies what captured signals are and how they contribute to tailoring future interactions.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This statement provides additional context about the role of captured signals in improving interaction effectiveness.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Vibe testing is a critical component of developing and validating artificial intelligence (LLM) models before deployment. It involves simulating user interactions with the model to ensure its performance, reliability, and correctness. Vibe testing helps identify bugs, optimize parameters, and improve the overall quality of the model. While vibe testing is essential for ensuring the robustness and accuracy of an LLM-based application, it alone is insufficient for deploying the model. To fully validate an LLM app, additional steps such as thorough testing, rigorous validation processes, and continuous monitoring are required.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what is vibe testing and why isn't it enough for deploying an llm app?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"Why do we need an offline eval? Why don't we make an LLM based software and deploy it directly? What is the problem? You have to tell me this. Actually, it's a very simple question. One very straightforward answer is that if we deploy it without testing, then how will the software behave in the production? We don't know. And anything can be risky. I had told you about two or three case studies in the beginning. Air Canada, Chart GPT. So, you remember, right? You cannot deploy an LLM based software into production without testing it. The first benefit of offline eval is that it gives us a feature of pre-release testing. Before releasing, I can properly test my eval. Now, let me tell you an interesting thing. You will probably read this in the course of LLM Ops. That the whole process of\", \"but it was a big blow on the name of a big company. How can you make such a mistake after being such a big company? So this case became very famous. And this case came in the picture because the developers here also did vibe testing. They did not properly test the chatbot and deployed it. So you can see how much loss can be reached on the scale of a big company if you launch an LLM based application without testing it. Similarly, there is one more incident which became very popular 2-3 years ago. There was a car dealership, an American brand called Chevrolet. They had a dealer. The dealer made a chatbot so that they could deal with customers. So a person went and started chatting with the chatbot. And he felt that there is a potential that I can do anything with this chatbot. So what he\", \"criteria. These three are very important pointers. Very important characteristics of LLM evals. Let's understand these. First of all let's talk about systematic. Systematic means that you are not doing vibe testing here. You got five questions in your mind. You asked and got the answer. You thought everything is fine. It doesn't happen. Here what you do is you create proper data sets. And in those data sets you try to cover all kinds of edge cases. So that you can properly test your chatbot or LLM based system. Okay. For example if I am creating a chatbot of campusX. Then what I will do is that I will randomly pick up what 100 real users are chatting with my system. And I will create a database of that chat. I will create a database. I will create a data set. And I will test on that data\", \"the same 5 questions, you will ask something else. So it is not even repeatable. Now you must be understanding even after listening to vibe testing that it works only at the personal project level. By doing vibe testing, you don't make production grade software in the industry. Obviously. Right? Now I will tell you some instances, some case studies where teams made a mistake that they deployed a LLME based application by doing vibe testing. And I will tell you the results with the help of 3 case studies. The first case study is Air Canada's chatbot. This was a very popular incident around 2-3 years ago. Where there was this guy and his grandmother died. And he was searching for bereavement fair on Air Canada's website. Bereavement fair is basically when someone in your family dies or your\", \"will he say. And how will your chatbot respond after turning it. You never know. And then there can be a lot of problems on the scale of a big company. So that is why one thing that you should take from the case studies of these three is that evaluation is super important. Without this, you cannot deploy your LLM based applications. One more question should come to your mind at this point. Because it came to my mind too. And that is that if the LLM application's evaluation is so important. Then why is this evaluation not so common? Why is there no other talk about it? Why don't people study? So the answer to this is that evaluating LLM based applications is not straightforward. It's not like a software application. Which you quickly write some test cases and evaluate. Evaluating LLM based\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 0.8125, "reason": "The score is 0.81 because the retrieval context contains information about teams deploying LLM-based applications without proper validation through veeb testing, which indirectly relates to the q