ers being exposed to potentially harmful content, the possibility of malicious actors exploiting vulnerabilities, and the potential for the system to become unstable due to incorrect or harmful outputs. On the other hand, quality issues are focused on verifying that the system produces accurate and useful results, free from errors, biases, or unfair treatment of individuals or groups. Tohru evaluates both\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how are safety issues like toxicity evaluated differently from quality issues?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"5 seconds to fetch it. It can also be a problem there. So basically what I am trying to say is that not only you have multiple failure points but associated with each failure point, you have multiple aspects to it which we call risk categories. So, we broadly divide risk categories into three parts. First is the quality of the application. Second is the safety related. And third is the operations related. Here I have written the definition of all three. The application quality tells us that the answer that comes out from the system is it good or not. Whether the app does its actual job well. It gives correct relevant complete answers which the user asked. So, this is the application quality. In safety, you ensure that the answer that comes out should not be harmful. Now, here you see\", \"In safety, you ensure that the answer that comes out should not be harmful. Now, here you see multiple things. You see that the answer should not be toxic. Toxic content should not be there. Dangerous content should not be there. Biased content should not be there. Private data should not leak. Or you should not be able to jailbreak it and do something that it should not do. All this comes under safety. And lastly, what comes in operations is whether when we deploy it, it can run fast, cheap and reliably or not. So, basically in these three categories, we organize all the risks. So, what I have done is I have made a kind of table where I have written all the risk categories which you will see in the future or use when you make your own application. So, in the application, you will see\", \"didn't have a plan to teach more than this. Let me know if you have any questions. Like one or two questions are coming. Suppose a dashboard faithfulness is 0.87 toxicity is 0.002 latency is 3. Is the bot good? I want to know how we judge by evaluating the dashboard. Shiv actually has a baseline of everything. And that baseline generally comes from offline evaluation. So you compare against that baseline. Suppose your baseline, like you gave an example faithfulness is 0.87 and your baseline is 0.85 So 0.87 is better than 0.85 so you will be happy. But suddenly in the last 24 hours faithfulness score went to 0.75 So against the 0.85 baseline, this is concerning. So you will be alerted or you will make some improvements in the system. That happens. Is the answer clear? So guys some\", \"which is of campusx teachers we will evaluate this also and you can do this so this also comes in application level evaluation so these are some quality related metrics apart from this, if you talk about application level then you will also test safety here you will check that your response is not toxic or you will check that your response is not giving any personally identifiable information you will check that your campusx downed solver can't be jailbreak so we will do these safety evaluations on step no.8 on step no.8 we will run safety evaluations on this application and lastly comes operations evaluations where you check 3 things primarily, your latency of the application, cost how much you are getting per query and how many tokens you are spending so this comes in step no.9 we call\", \"you see here there is an option called evaluators? See. Here get started with evaluators is one. Go to show all templates. From here you can actually go through many options. Like there are different evaluators for security. And see it is written next to each one. LLM as a judge. LLM as a judge. It means that now this one what is its function? PII leakage. It computes and tells us that our chat bot is not leaking any kind of personal information. And how does this evaluator work? Behind the scenes LLM as a judge is sitting. Prompt injection is different for detecting. Code injection is different for detecting. In safety it is different for toxicity. It is different for bias and fairness. Talking about quality hallucination has a different evaluation pipeline. Correctness is different.\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 0.7857142857142857, "reason": "The score is 0.79 because the retrieved context does not provide specific information about evaluating safety issues like toxicity differently from quality issues, which is crucial for understanding the evaluation process described in the input. The context lacks direct references to these topics, making it less relevant to the query.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Verdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdicts\": [\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Safety issues like toxicity are evaluated differently from quality issues.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": \"\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Quality issues are evaluated based on criteria such as user experience, design standards, and compliance with industry regulations.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n                \"reason\": \"\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"statement\": \"Safety issues are evaluated based on criteria such as preventing harm to users, ensuring data privacy, and complying with legal requirements.\",\n                \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n            