en/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"The reason why we can't test an LLM app the same way we test normal software is because LLMs (Large Language Models) rely heavily on their ability to generate human-like text, which makes them inherently complex and difficult to evaluate. Unlike traditional software, where developers can easily replicate the behavior of a program by writing test cases, LLMs require specialized training and algorithms to simulate natural language processing. This means that LLMs cannot be tested in the same way as regular software, which relies on predefined test cases and scripts. Additionally, LLMs often require extensive fine-tuning and optimization to achieve optimal performance, making it\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"Normal software is deterministic and rule-based, so the same input always gives the same output and you can assert one exact expected value. LLM applications are probabilistic, so the same input can produce different wordings, and most tasks have many acceptable answers instead of one exact string. That breaks exact-match testing, so LLM evaluation instead judges qualities of the output, usually against a golden dataset and often with an LLM as a judge.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"why can't we test an LLM app the same way we test normal software?\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Correctness [GEval]", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.0, "reason": "The actual output does not address the question about why we can't test LLM apps the same way we test normal software.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Criteria:\nNone \n \nEvaluation Steps:\n[\n    \"Compare the actual answer with the expected answer.\",\n    \"Treat the expected answer as the reference answer.\",\n    \"Check whether the 