reshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false, "evaluation_steps": ["Judge whether the answer is clear and understandable.", "Check whether the answer directly addresses the student's question.", "Check whether the answer uses a professional and appropriate teaching tone.", "Do not judge factual correctness or completeness.", "Judge writing style only."], "evaluation_params": ["input", "actual_output"]}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"The distinction between reference-based and reference-free evaluations lies in whether the correct answer is known beforehand or not. In reference-based evaluations, the correctness of the answer is determined solely by the output produced by the model during the evaluation process. On the other hand, reference-free evaluations involve evaluating the performance of the model against a predefined set of correct answers, ensuring that the model produces outputs consistent with those expected by the evaluators.\\nReference-based evaluations are typically conducted using metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score. These metrics are calculated based on the model's predictions and compared against the true labels to assess its performance. The goal\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"A reference-based eval needs a ground-truth answer to compare against, a golden or expected output authored ahead of time, and scores the system by how close it comes to that reference. A reference-free eval needs no ground truth and instead judges an intrinsic property of the output, usually with an LLM as a judge. The test is simply whether the metric requires an expected answer.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what does reference-based vs reference-free eval mean?\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Correctness [GEval]", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 0.9, "reason": "The actual output provides a detailed 