tten, unprofessional, or difficult to understand.\n5-8: The answer is generally clear and professional but has minor style or clarity issues.\n9-10: The answer is clear, concise, professional, and easy to understand. \n \nScore: 0.0"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false, "evaluation_steps": ["Judge whether the answer is clear and understandable.", "Check whether the answer directly addresses the student's question.", "Check whether the answer uses a professional and appropriate teaching tone.", "Do not judge factual correctness or completeness.", "Judge writing style only."], "evaluation_params": ["input", "actual_output"]}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Drift refers to the phenomenon where the results of past experiments or tests do not accurately reflect the true state of the system. After deployment, drift can occur due to several reasons:\\n\\n1. **Systematic Failures**: As mentioned in the previous response, there can be systematic failures in production that were not anticipated during the initial testing phase. For instance, sudden shifts in user demographics (e.g., millennials vs. Gen Z) could lead to drift in the data.\\n\\n2. **Data Drift**: Data drift occurs when the data collected during the offline evaluation process becomes outdated or inaccurate over time. This can happen due to various factors such\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"Drift is the gradual degradation of a deployed system's performance over time as real-world data or usage patterns move away from what the system was built and evaluated on. Users may start asking new kinds of questions or the knowledge base may go stale, so metrics slowly fall even though the code did not change. It matters because these failures appear silently in production, so you monitor metrics over time, set alerts when they drop, and feed failing cases back into your offline dataset.\", \"hyperparameters\"