{"test_cases_lookup_map": {"{\"actual_output\": \"The main difference between model evals and application evals lies in their purpose and approach. \\n\\nModel evals are designed to assess the performance of machine learning (LLM) models against predefined benchmarks. These benchmarks typically consist of various datasets and algorithms that are commonly used in the field of machine learning. By running these evaluations, developers can determine whether a particular model meets specific criteria or if it performs well within certain parameters. This type of evaluation helps ensure that the model is robust and reliable.\\n\\nApplication evals, on the other hand, are specifically tailored for evaluating the functionality and performance of software applications built using machine learning technologies. These applications often\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"Expected action: ANSWER\\n\\nSuccess criteria:\\nProvides a substantive answer to the course-related question rather than refusing or changing the topic.\\n\\nImportant:\\nThe expected action is a behavioral label.\\nDo not require the actual response to literally contain\\nANSWER, DECLINE, or PARTIAL.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"What is the difference between model evals and application evals?\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Scope Adherence [GEval]", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The output correctly identifies the difference between model evals and application evals without deviating into unrelated content.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct", "evaluationCost": 0, "verboseLogs": "Criteria:\nNone \n \nEvaluation Steps:\n[\n    \"Treat the expected action and success criteria as ground truth.\",\n    \"The expected action is a behavioral label, not text that must literally appear in the answer.\",\n    \"If the expected action is ANSWER, check whether t